  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Let $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$. Find the largest possible value of the greatest common divisor of the numbers $5n+6$ and $8n+7$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$13$.\n\nEvery common divisor divides $8(5n+6)-5(8n+7)=13$, and $n=4$ attains this value.",
      "worked": "Let $d=\\operatorname{GCD}(5n+6,8n+7)$. A common divisor divides integer multiples, their sums and their differences. Thus $d$ divides\n$$\\begin{align*}8(5n+6)-5(8n+7)&=40n+48-40n-35\\\\8(5n+6)-5(8n+7)&=13.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $d\\leq13$. For $n=4$,\n$$\\begin{align*}5n+6&=26,\\\\8n+7&=39,\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(26,39)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(26,13)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(26,39)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(13,13)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(26,39)&=13.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, the largest possible greatest common divisor is $13$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 92
  },
